  values ('ME', 'analytical', 'Bhutan is currently confronting a demographic challenge characterised by a declining fertility rate, which has decreased from 6.6 births per woman in 1971 to 2.0 in recent years, below the replacement level of 2.1. Despite some recent positive trends and government initiatives, reversing this decline is a multifaceted issue influenced by various socioeconomic factors.

a) Critically examine the underlying causes and potential consequences of Bhutan''s declining fertility rate, and discuss its impact on the country''s socioeconomic development. (10 marks)', 'a) Causes: (1) more girls are educated and women work outside the home, so they marry later and have fewer children; (2) urbanisation and the high cost of housing, child care and education make large families expensive; (3) access to family planning and good health care has reduced the need for many children (child survival is high); (4) aspirations have changed - couples prefer fewer children and focus on careers and quality of life; (5) many young people of reproductive age have emigrated (e.g. to Australia).
Consequences and impact: Bhutan''s population will age; the workforce will shrink, leading to labour shortages in agriculture, construction and services; the dependency ratio will rise as fewer workers support more elderly people, increasing the cost of health care, pensions and elderly care; economic growth may slow; rural villages will empty and schools may close; and for a small country, a declining population raises concerns about national security, identity and sustainability. On the positive side, fewer children may allow more investment per child in health and education.', null, '[{"point": "Causes: education and employment of women, later marriage, urbanisation, high cost of living/ housing and child rearing, family planning access, changing aspirations, emigration of young people, career focus, smaller family preference", "marks": 4}, {"point": "Consequences and impact: ageing population, shrinking workforce and labour shortages, higher dependency and elderly care costs, slower economic growth, pressure on pension and health, rural decline, school closures, national security/ identity concerns for a small population", "marks": 6}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development') and t.slug = 'population-and-demography'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-general-3'), 'standard', 10,
