  values ('ME', 'analytical', 'Bhutan is currently confronting a demographic challenge characterised by a declining fertility rate, which has decreased from 6.6 births per woman in 1971 to 2.0 in recent years, below the replacement level of 2.1. Despite some recent positive trends and government initiatives, reversing this decline is a multifaceted issue influenced by various socioeconomic factors.

b) In your opinion, what effective measures could be implemented to address this demographic challenge? (10 marks)', 'b) Measures: (1) family-friendly policies - extended paid maternity and paternity leave (Bhutan already extended maternity leave to six months for civil servants), flexible working hours and work-from-home options; (2) affordable, quality child care centres at workplaces and in communities; (3) financial support - child allowances, tax relief, housing support and education subsidies for families with children; (4) good maternal and child health services and fertility treatment; (5) create jobs, good pay and opportunities at home to reduce emigration of young people, and encourage those abroad to return; (6) affordable housing for young couples; (7) promote gender equality in sharing household and child care responsibilities; (8) prepare for ageing through elderly care and productivity-raising technology. Lessons from countries such as Japan and South Korea show that incentives alone are not enough; a supportive environment for raising children is essential.', null, '[{"point": "Measures: family-friendly policies (longer maternity/ paternity leave, child care centres, flexible work), financial incentives (child allowances, tax relief, housing), affordable health and fertility services, work-life balance, retain/ attract youth (reduce emigration), support for young couples, awareness; learning from other countries", "marks": 10}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
