  values ('ME', 'analytical', 'According to the National Statistics Bureau''s first quarterly Labour Force Survey Report 2024, the youth unemployment rate reached 22.9% in the first quarter of the year, up from 15.9% in the previous quarter. Notably, unemployment among young males is higher at 25.7%, compared with 19.7% for young females.

a) What are the potential socio-economic implications of the rising youth unemployment rate on the country''s economic development? (10 marks)', 'a) Socio-economic implications: (1) Lost human capital and productivity - educated young people who are not working do not contribute to output, slowing GDP growth and reducing tax revenue. (2) Wasted public investment in education. (3) Increased dependency on parents and families, reducing household savings. (4) Emigration - many unemployed youth go abroad, causing brain drain and labour shortages in key sectors. (5) Social problems - frustration, depression and other mental health problems, substance abuse and crime increase among idle youth. (6) Delayed marriage and family formation, contributing to the falling fertility rate. (7) Loss of trust in government and in the value of education. (8) Pressure on the government to create civil service jobs it cannot afford.', null, '[{"point": "Economic: wasted human capital and lost productivity, lower growth and tax revenue, return on education investment lost, more dependency on families, emigration/ brain drain", "marks": 5}, {"point": "Social: frustration and mental health problems, substance abuse and crime, rural-urban/ overseas migration, delayed marriage and family formation, loss of trust in government", "marks": 5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
