  values ('ME', 'analytical', 'According to the National Statistics Bureau''s first quarterly Labour Force Survey Report 2024, the youth unemployment rate reached 22.9% in the first quarter of the year, up from 15.9% in the previous quarter. Notably, unemployment among young males is higher at 25.7%, compared with 19.7% for young females.

b) How might the gender disparity in youth unemployment affect social stability and economic growth in the long term? (10 marks)', 'b) Gender disparity: young men (25.7%) are more unemployed than young women (19.7%). Social stability: idle young men are more likely to become involved in substance abuse, crime, violence and gang activity, threatening public safety; alienation and loss of self-worth can increase family conflict; many may emigrate, changing the gender balance in communities. Economic growth: it may reflect a mismatch between the skills and courses young men choose and the jobs available (e.g. in services where women find more jobs), or reluctance to take blue-collar jobs; unless addressed, the economy loses the contribution of a large group, household incomes fall, and dependency rises. Policies should study the causes and target both genders: technical and vocational training linked to market needs, entrepreneurship support, private sector job creation, apprenticeships, changing attitudes towards blue-collar work, and counselling and career guidance.', null, '[{"point": "Effects of higher male youth unemployment: crime, substance abuse, violence, alienation of young men, family stress, social instability; male emigration; gender roles changing", "marks": 5}, {"point": "Long-term growth: skills mismatch, need to understand reasons (education choices, sectors), wasted potential, lower household incomes; balanced policies for both genders - skills, TVET, entrepreneurship, private sector jobs", "marks": 5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
