with nq as (
  insert into public.questions (stage, question_type, stem, model_answer, explanation, key_points, subject_id, topic_id, paper_id, difficulty, marks,
                                estimated_seconds, origin, status, parent_question_id, variation_type, ai_review, passage_group, passage_position, verified_by, subject_group_id, specialisation_id, image_url)
  values ('ME', 'analytical', 'The 13th Five Year Plan (July 2024 to June 2029) has adopted a 10-year strategic framework to support the nation''s long-term goal of becoming a High Income GNH Economy by 2034 - driven by the interconnected pillars of people, progress and prosperity.

b) Analyse the potential factors that have led Bhutan to prioritise this ambitious goal in its development strategy. (15 marks)', 'b) Factors behind this priority:
1. His Majesty''s vision: His Majesty has called for Bhutan to become a developed nation within our lifetime, and the Gelephu Mindfulness City initiative reflects this ambition.
2. Graduation from Least Developed Country status (December 2023): Bhutan will lose concessional aid and trade benefits, so it must grow its own economy.
3. Youth unemployment and emigration: high youth unemployment and large numbers of educated Bhutanese leaving for better pay abroad show that the economy must create more and better-paid jobs to retain talent.
4. Slow growth and economic vulnerabilities: COVID-19, fiscal deficits, rising public debt, pressure on foreign currency reserves and a narrow economic base dependent on hydropower and imports call for diversification and higher productivity.
5. Rising aspirations and cost of living: citizens expect better incomes, housing and services.
6. Demographic change: a falling fertility rate and ageing population require higher productivity per worker.
7. Global competition and technology: the digital economy and green industries offer new opportunities a small country can seize.
The ''GNH'' part ensures that the pursuit of high income does not sacrifice the environment, culture and wellbeing that make Bhutan unique.', null, '[{"point": "Factors: LDC graduation (December 2023) and need to sustain growth; youth unemployment and emigration/ brain drain - need good jobs and incomes; slow growth and economic shocks (COVID, fiscal deficits, debt, reserves); over-dependence on hydropower and aid; His Majesty''s vision (developed nation in our lifetime, GMC); rising aspirations and cost of living; demographic change; global competitiveness/ technology", "marks": 12}, {"point": "Balanced analysis: ambitious but GNH ensures growth is not at the cost of environment/ culture", "marks": 3}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
