  values ('ME', 'analytical', 'According to the Public Debt Situation Report for the quarter ending 30 June 2024, total public debt stock reached Nu 285 billion, a decrease of almost Nu 8 billion compared with Nu 293 billion as of 31 March 2024. The debt comprises external debt of over Nu 263 billion and domestic debt of over Nu 21 billion. External debt makes up 92.5 percent of the total.

b) Considering that external debt constitutes 92.5 percent of total public debt as of 30 June 2024, discuss the potential risks and challenges this high dependency on external debt poses for the country. How should the government address these challenges to ensure long-term economic stability? (15 marks)', 'b) Risks and challenges of high external debt (92.5%): (1) most external debt is linked to hydropower projects; repayment depends on hydropower revenue, which is affected by rainfall, project delays and cost overruns (e.g. Punatsangchhu I and II); (2) exchange rate risk - debt in US dollars and other convertible currencies becomes more expensive if the ngultrum depreciates; (3) pressure on foreign currency reserves (both rupee and convertible currency) when servicing debt; (4) changes in global interest rates and the loss of concessional terms after LDC graduation; (5) high debt service reduces funds available for health, education and other development; (6) dependence on donors and lenders can limit policy independence; (7) vulnerability to external shocks such as pandemics and global crises.
How to address them: borrow prudently within the Public Debt Policy thresholds and mainly on concessional terms; invest borrowed funds only in productive, revenue-generating projects; complete hydropower projects on time and within budget; diversify the economy and exports (tourism, manufacturing, IT, agriculture) to earn foreign exchange; build adequate foreign reserves; improve domestic revenue through tax reform and efficient collection; develop the domestic debt market to reduce reliance on external borrowing; manage currency risk; and maintain transparent debt reporting and strong debt management.', null, '[{"point": "Risks: most external debt is hydropower-related (in INR from Government of India) - repayment depends on hydropower revenue and rainfall/ project delays; exchange rate risk (USD/ other currencies), foreign currency reserve pressure, rising interest rates, debt service burden reducing spending on social sectors, dependence on donors, vulnerability to shocks", "marks": 8}, {"point": "Measures: prudent borrowing within debt policy limits, concessional loans, timely completion of hydropower projects, diversify revenue and exports, build reserves, improve tax collection, develop domestic debt market, debt management and transparency, use loans for productive investment", "marks": 7}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development') and t.slug = 'public-debt-and-the-economy'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-general-3'), 'standard', 15,
    1620, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
    '{"verdict": "pass", "issues": [], "checker_notes": "Model answer, marking guide and rubric written by Claude from the RCSC paper; facts were checked against what is widely published, but please read before publishing. Real past-paper questions keep RCSC wording.", "batch": "ME Paper III (General): BCSE 2024, batch 2", "source": {"title": "BCSE 2024 Main Examination, General category, Paper III", "year": 2024, "url": "https://rcsc.gov.bt/bcse-past-question-papers/"}}'::jsonb, 'me-general-3-2024b2-b5', 2, null, null, null, null)
